[?2004huser@169be8705533:~/pentest$ cat > finding.txt << 'EOF'
[?2004l[?2004h> FINDING: HTTP/HTTPS Traffic Blocking Issue
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> AFFECTED RULES:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 7: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m string --string "Mozilla" --algo bm -j ACCEPT
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 8: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -m string --string "Mozilla" --algo bm -j ACCEPT
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 9: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 10: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j DROP
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> The iptables ruleset implements a flawed User-Agent filtering mechanism. Rules 7-8 only accept HTTP/HTTPS traffic if the packet contains the string "Mozilla" (attempting to filter by browser type). Rules 9-10 then explicitly DROP all other HTTP/HTTPS traffic that doesn't match.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> WHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:
[?2004l[?2004h> - SSH (port 22): Has an unconditional ACCEPT rule with no string matching requirements
[?2004l[?2004h> - HTTP/HTTPS (ports 80/443): Require packets to contain "Mozilla" string to be accepted
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> WHY CURL HANGS:
[?2004l[?2004h> The curl command sends a User-Agent header like "curl/7.x.x" which does not contain the string "Mozilla". Therefore:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. The packet doesn't match rules 7-8 (no "Mozilla" string found)
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. The packet hits rules 9-10 and gets DROPped (not REJECTed)
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Since DROP silently discards packets, curl waits indefinitely for a response that never comes
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> RECOMMENDATION:
[?2004l[?2004h> This string-matching approach for access control is fundamentally flawed:
[?2004l[?2004h> - It can be trivially bypassed by adding "Mozilla" to any User-Agent
[?2004l[?2004h> - It blocks legitimate tools (curl, wget, scripts, monitoring systems, APIs)
[?2004l[?2004h> - String matching on packet payload is CPU-intensive and unreliable
[?2004l[?2004h> - It provides no real security benefit
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The organization should remove rules 7-10 and implement proper access controls using:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Source IP whitelisting (-s flag)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Network-based restrictions (firewall zones)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Application-layer authentication (not firewall rules)
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004huser@169be8705533:~/pentest$ cat finding.txt
[?2004lFINDING: HTTP/HTTPS Traffic Blocking Issue

AFFECTED RULES:
- Rule 7: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m string --string "Mozilla" --algo bm -j ACCEPT
- Rule 8: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -m string --string "Mozilla" --algo bm -j ACCEPT
- Rule 9: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP
- Rule 10: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j DROP

ROOT CAUSE:
The iptables ruleset implements a flawed User-Agent filtering mechanism. Rules 7-8 only accept HTTP/HTTPS traffic if the packet contains the string "Mozilla" (attempting to filter by browser type). Rules 9-10 then explicitly DROP all other HTTP/HTTPS traffic that doesn't match.

WHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:
- SSH (port 22): Has an unconditional ACCEPT rule with no string matching requirements
- HTTP/HTTPS (ports 80/443): Require packets to contain "Mozilla" string to be accepted

WHY CURL HANGS:
The curl command sends a User-Agent header like "curl/7.x.x" which does not contain the string "Mozilla". Therefore:
1. The packet doesn't match rules 7-8 (no "Mozilla" string found)
2. The packet hits rules 9-10 and gets DROPped (not REJECTed)
3. Since DROP silently discards packets, curl waits indefinitely for a response that never comes

RECOMMENDATION:
This string-matching approach for access control is fundamentally flawed:
- It can be trivially bypassed by adding "Mozilla" to any User-Agent
- It blocks legitimate tools (curl, wget, scripts, monitoring systems, APIs)
- String matching on packet payload is CPU-intensive and unreliable
- It provides no real security benefit

The organization should remove rules 7-10 and implement proper access controls using:
- Source IP whitelisting (-s flag)
- Network-based restrictions (firewall zones)
- Application-layer authentication (not firewall rules)
